  "title": "<p class=\"ql-align-center\"><strong style=\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(128, 100, 162);\">The Effect of Surface Nanostructure on Clean Energy Catalyst Performance</strong></p><p><br></p><p><br></p>",
  "abstract": "<p>Electrocatalytic materials are instrumental for enabling fast reactions in clean energy devices. One especially promising candidate is cerium oxide (ceria). Much excitement has surrounded the computational prediction that the catalytic performance of ceria might be improved by tuning its surface nanostructure but the theory has yet to be validated. This project experimentally investigates ceria’s catalytic activity by comparing how well its varying surface orientations facilitate oxygen reduction reactions. ECR process design yields insights about the experimentally-sensitive solid state physics assumptions <span style=\"color: rgb(34, 34, 34);\">and establishes a framework for kinetic investigations of similar clean energy materials. </span>In quantifying reactivity through electrical conductivity relaxation (ECR), our data suggests that there is not a significant difference between orientations. </p>",
